---
description: A component was referenced by name, but that name is not in the grid's registered components.
---

## Component Name Not Registered

A column or grid option asked for a component by name — {% errorParam name="propertyName" fallback="cellRenderer" /%} was set to the string {% errorParam name="componentName" fallback="StatusRenderer" /%} — and the grid could not find that name when it came to render. The cell falls back to the grid's default component.

This message means the name is neither one of the grid's own components nor one you have registered. It is worth knowing that the grid tells those two cases apart: if you name a _built-in_ component whose module is not registered, you get [error #200](./errors/200) naming the module instead. So arriving here means the grid believes this is your component.

That leaves two causes, and the message's `Did you mean` suggestions usually settle which:

- **A typo, or a mismatch in case.** Names are matched exactly, so `statusRenderer` and `StatusRenderer` are different components.
- **The component was never registered**, or was registered on a different grid than the one rendering this column.

### Register it by name

Names are resolved from the `components` grid option, which maps each name to its implementation. The name in the map must match the string on the column exactly.

```{% frameworkTransform=true spaceBetweenProperties=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    components: {
        StatusRenderer: StatusRenderer,
    },
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'status', cellRenderer: 'StatusRenderer' },
    ],
}
```

See [Registering Custom Components](./components/#registering-custom-components) for the full detail, including the framework-specific forms.

### Or skip the name entirely

Referencing the component directly avoids this class of error, because a wrong name becomes a compile-time or import error rather than something discovered at render:

```{% frameworkTransform=true spaceBetweenProperties=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'status', cellRenderer: StatusRenderer },
    ],
}
```

Registering by name is still worth it when the name has to come from configuration or data — from a saved layout, or a server response — since a string is all you have in that case.

### If several grids need it

The `components` option applies to one grid. A grid that defines its own `components` map does not see another grid's. To make a component available to every grid, register it through [Global Grid Options](./grid-interface/#global-grid-options) with the `deep` merge strategy, so a grid's own map is merged with the global one rather than replacing it. All component types share one registry, so names must be unique across it.
